<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  `ld` — the first two letters of the name.

  Both are drawn with a ruler rather than taken from a font. The first version used
  the real DejaVu Sans Mono Bold `l`, and it was wrong on two counts: that glyph has
  a curved tail and a flag at the top, which at icon size reads as a smudge, and a
  filled glyph beside a stroked one does not render the same weight even when the
  numbers match. Both letters are single-weight strokes and both are straight. This
  is a terminal's letterform, which is what the app is.

  CORRECTED 2026-09-18. The previous revision had two defects, both arithmetic
  rather than taste, and both fixed here.

  (1) The letters did not share a baseline. The `d`'s bowl ended in a 51.4-degree
  miter whose outer point reached 10.8 units below the `l`'s foot, and that spike
  was the lowest ink in the mark. The comment claimed "both letters share the
  font's baseline (0)" while the drawing did not, and it was also what dragged the
  ink's centre off the icon's centre. Both letters now end their ink on y=85.

  (2) The `d` had NO COUNTER, and that is why the mark read as runes rather than
  letters. At a stroked corner the inner boundary is set back along the bisector by
  halfWidth/sin(theta/2), not by the half-width. The old bowl was a triangle of
  inradius 8.616 units; its apex (77.3 degrees) set back 8.325 and its bottom join
  (51.4 degrees) set back 12.001, which is larger than the inradius, so the hole
  closed to about 0.29 units — 0.37 px of radius on a 192 px icon. A `d` with no
  hole is a bar with a wedge, which is a rune. The bowl is now squared, with 90-degree
  corners where it meets the stem, and chamfered 45 degrees at its two outer corners.

  The chamfer is the "fancy" dial and it has a hard stop: the counter's left edge is
  exactly 31.6 - 2*chamfer units long, so a chamfer at or above 15.8 closes the
  aperture again and the rune comes back. 8 leaves 15.6 units, half the bowl's height.

  Geometry, all pre-transform in the 108 viewport: ink spans x 23..85 and y 23..85,
  so it is 62 x 62 and its centre is exactly (54,54). Centring had to be fixed BEFORE
  the group scale, because scaling about a pivot maps the ink centre c to 54 + s(c-54)
  and therefore preserves a centring error rather than removing it — the old mark was
  drawn 5.2% right and 5.9% low because of this. Both letters share one baseline at 85
  and one ascender at 23, so nothing hangs below anything.

  The stroke is 13.0 over an ink height of 62, a ratio of 0.2097 — the same weight
  ratio the previous revision used (10.4/49.82 = 0.2087), which is the font's own stem
  ratio (293/1556 = 0.188) with the ~10% optical correction for a stroke rather than a
  fill. Keeping the ratio is what stops the pair looking like two drawings.

  Everything sits inside the middle 66%: post-scale the ink spans 31.7..76.3 against a
  safe zone of 21..87 and a visible zone of 18..90, and its furthest point is 22.3 from
  the centre against a 33-unit safe radius, so no mask shape clips it — square, round,
  or the monochrome silhouette, where a real counter matters most because that mode
  discards the colour and renders alpha only.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="108dp"
    android:height="108dp"
    android:viewportWidth="108"
    android:viewportHeight="108">
    <group
        android:pivotX="54"
        android:pivotY="54"
        android:scaleX="0.72"
        android:scaleY="0.72">
        <!-- the l: one straight stem, ascender to baseline, no tail and no flag.
             Its ink is 13 wide; butt caps give it a flat face at each end. -->
        <path
            android:pathData="M29.5,23 L29.5,85"
            android:strokeColor="#EDEDED"
            android:strokeWidth="13"
            android:strokeLineCap="butt"
            android:strokeLineJoin="miter" />
        <!-- the d: the stem, then a squared bowl chamfered 45 degrees at its two outer
             corners. One path, no cancelling strokes, so every join is a real miter.

             It is squared rather than chevroned because a chevron's two acute corners
             each set their inner boundary back further than the counter's own
             inradius, which closes the hole. The 90-degree corners where the bowl's
             runs meet the stem set back only halfWidth (6.5), and the 135-degree
             chamfers set back 1.082 x halfWidth, so the counter survives with an area
             of about 221 units pre-scale — roughly 15 x 24 px on a 192 px icon.

             The bottom-right corner is 90 degrees, so its miter's outer point is
             exactly (85,85): the ink bottom is the baseline with no spike below it.

             Left open on purpose. Closing it with Z gives the identical silhouette,
             because the top run's butt face is vertical at x=85, flush with the stem's
             right edge, but a Z would create a 180-degree direction reversal at the
             stem's tip, which is a degenerate join. -->
        <path
            android:pathData="M78.5,23 L78.5,78.5 L61.5,78.5 L53.5,70.5 L53.5,54.9 L61.5,46.9 L78.5,46.9"
            android:strokeColor="#EDEDED"
            android:strokeWidth="13"
            android:strokeLineCap="butt"
            android:strokeLineJoin="miter"
            android:strokeMiterLimit="3" />
    </group>
</vector>